<h2>Where BWP Is Overkill — and What to Use Instead</h2>
<p>Using BWP everywhere is like wearing a raincoat indoors. It works, but you're paying for protection you don't need. Here's where to downgrade intelligently:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Bedroom wardrobes (dry interior):</strong> Use BWR (IS 303 Type 2). Saves ₹1,200–₹1,800 per sheet. Over 8–10 sheets for a master wardrobe, that's ₹10,000–₹18,000 saved — with identical 15-year lifespan in dry conditions.</li>
  <li><strong>Study table / bookshelf:</strong> BWR or even MR (IS 303 Type 1) if budget is very tight. These units see zero water and minimal humidity variation.</li>
  <li><strong>Walk-in closets:</strong> BWR. The room is air-conditioned and climate-controlled. BWP's moisture advantage is irrelevant here.</li>
  <li><strong>Entertainment units / TV walls:</strong> BWR. Behind a TV in an air-conditioned living room is the driest spot in the house.</li>
  <li><strong>Loft storage:</strong> BWR or MR. Loft units are near the ceiling in the hottest, driest zone. Moisture is never a concern.</li>
</ol>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span><strong>The hybrid-approach savings (real 2BHK example):</strong><p><strong>All-BWP 2BHK:</strong> ~45 sheets × ₹4,800 avg = ₹2,16,000 in plywood <br> <strong>Hybrid (BWP kitchen + bathroom, BWR everything else):</strong> 15 sheets BWP × ₹4,800 + 30 sheets BWR × ₹3,200 = ₹1,68,000 <br> <strong>Savings: ₹48,000</strong> — redirectable to better hardware, acrylic shutters or a full cove-ceiling. Zero lifespan compromise.</div>
